    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">How Eco-Friendly Is a Boat Trip in Madeira? An Honest Answer</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>18 November 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Not very, if we are honest. A motor boat burns fuel, and no trip on one is low-impact. What changes the harm is how the boat is run: how fast it goes, how it treats wildlife, what goes into the sea, and how many people share the trip.</p>

      <h2>Is a small boat worse than a big one per guest?</h2><p>Often, yes. A large catamaran carrying dozens of people spreads its fuel over many more guests than a small private boat with five on board. Count fuel per person and nothing else, and the big boat usually comes out ahead.</p><p>But fuel per person is not the whole story. Any motor boat burns fuel much faster at high speed than when it cruises gently, so how a trip is driven matters as much as the size of the boat. Chifbay's boat is a 9 metre motor boat for up to five guests. The Day Trip ends with a fast run back to Funchal, and nobody should pretend that part is green. There is no published fuel figure for our boat, so this guide does not invent one. Be wary of any operator who quotes a carbon number without saying how it was measured.</p><h2>What matters more than the size of the boat?</h2><p>For wildlife, the driving matters more than the boat. The real damage a tourist boat can do here is chasing dolphins, cutting across a group, or crowding a seal that is trying to rest. Madeira's law on this is strict: a boat may not steer in to within 50 metres of a dolphin, whale or seal, may spend only 10 minutes in the watching zone, and may never chase the animals or put swimmers in with them. A small boat that keeps to those limits does less harm than a large one that ignores them, and the reverse is just as true.</p><p>Noise counts too. Under Cabo Girão, inside the marine park, music or noise loud enough to disturb wildlife or other people needs a permit. Our <a href="/posts/rules-for-watching-dolphins-in-madeira">guide to the dolphin watching rules</a> has the full detail.</p><h2>Does sunscreen harm the sea in Madeira?</h2><p>Possibly, though less is known here than for tropical reefs. Hawaii banned the sale of sunscreens containing oxybenzone and octinoxate from 1 January 2021, after research linked them to coral damage. Madeira has no coral reefs, and the evidence for rocky temperate coasts like this one is thinner. Still, whatever is on your skin washes off at every swim stop, on every trip, every day of the summer.</p><p>The easy fix is to need less of it. Wear a long-sleeved UV shirt and a hat, sit in the shade between swims, and put sunscreen only on the skin still exposed. Mineral sunscreens based on zinc oxide usually contain neither of the two chemicals Hawaii banned. Put it on before you leave the pontoon so it has time to settle before you jump in. For UV levels month by month, see <a href="/posts/how-strong-is-the-sun-on-a-boat-in-madeira">sun strength on a boat in Madeira</a>.</p><h2>What happens to rubbish on a boat?</h2><p>The rule is simple: nothing goes over the side. In the Cabo Girão park, throwing out solid or liquid waste that could harm the sea is banned outright. On a small boat the real risk is not carelessness but wind. A napkin, a plastic cup or a cap can blow away in a second when the boat picks up speed.</p><p>Food and drinks are served on board on every Chifbay trip, so there is always something light around. Hold on to it when the skipper speeds up, keep loose things in a bag, and if something does fly off, say so straight away. A cap in the water is easy to pick up if the boat turns at once, and much harder a minute later.</p><h2>Is a private trip better or worse than a shared tour?</h2><p>It depends on what you measure. On fuel per person, a full shared boat wins. On pressure at each stop, a small group is lighter: five people swimming at Fajã dos Padres is not the same as sixty. A private boat can also slow down, wait or leave a crowded spot without a schedule pushing it on. Our comparison of a <a href="/posts/private-boat-charter-vs-shared-tour-madeira">private charter and a shared tour in Madeira</a> covers the other differences.</p><p>There is also a label worth knowing. In 2026, seven boats in Madeira held a Blue Flag for eco-tourism, alongside 17 beaches and three marinas. A flag is not the only sign of a careful operator, but it is fair to ask any company which standard it follows and how its crew handle wildlife.</p><p>Now the uncomfortable part. If the lowest possible footprint is your main goal, a boat trip is not the answer. Walk a levada, take the bus to Câmara de Lobos, or paddle a kayak along the cliffs. A boat trip is a treat. The fair question is not whether it is green, but whether it is run with care.</p><h2>What can you do as a guest?</h2><p>Quite a lot, and none of it is hard:</p><ol><li>Never ask the skipper to get closer to dolphins, whales, turtles or seals. Let the animals choose.</li><li>Cover up with a shirt and hat so you need less sunscreen, and choose a mineral one.</li><li>Hold on to napkins, cups and caps whenever the boat speeds up.</li><li>Take nothing alive from the sea, and do not feed the fish.</li><li>If you see an injured animal or a turtle tangled in rope, tell the skipper. Madeira's wildlife network, Rede SOS Vida Selvagem, has a WhatsApp line for this: +351 961 957 545.</li><li>If footprint matters to you, pick a shorter route. The 2 hour <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a> turns at Cabo Girão and covers less water than the longer trips.</li></ol><p>Whichever you choose, both of our <a href="/experiences">trips from Marina do Funchal</a> follow the same coast west, and the same rules apply on each of them.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is a sailing catamaran greener than a motor boat?</h3><p>Only when it is actually under sail. Ask how much of the route is sailed and how much is motored.</p><h3>Do boat trips in Madeira disturb dolphins?</h3><p>They can, which is why Madeira bans active approaches closer than 50 metres, sets speed limits near the animals and allows each boat 10 minutes in the watching zone.</p><h3>What sunscreen is best for swimming in the sea?</h3><p>A water-resistant mineral sunscreen based on zinc oxide, used with a UV shirt and a hat so you need less of it.</p><h3>Does a private boat trip use more fuel than a shared tour?</h3><p>Per person, usually yes, because fewer guests share the fuel. Per stop, a small group puts less pressure on swim spots and wildlife.</p>

      <blockquote>The sea does not grade intentions. It only sees the speed, the distance and what goes over the side.</blockquote>
    </article>
